{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChK7GVuoYZkn1AbHoFjKm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the crash in `django/contrib/auth/migrations/0011_update_proxy_permissions.py`. The `auth_tests` suite passes (497 tests, 7 skipped). No test covers the exact case from the issue, so the fix itself hasn't been run against it.\n\n**Cause:** for each proxy model, the migration moves its permissions from the concrete model's content type to the proxy's own content type. If a model was recreated as a proxy, permissions with the same codenames may already exist under the proxy's content type. The move then hits the unique constraint on `(content_type_id, codename)` and the whole migration fails.\n\n**Fix:** the move for each proxy model now runs in its own `transaction.atomic()` block, inside a `try/except IntegrityError`.\n- **On a collision,** only that model's move is rolled back. The migration prints a warning naming the old and new content types and the codenames involved, and asks the user to check those permissions.\n- **Everything else** still migrates normally, and the same handling covers the reverse migration.\n\nThe migration doesn't decide which permission to keep when duplicates exist. Those rows are left untouched for the user to sort out by hand."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":161,"cache_read_input_tokens":21993,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":161},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6eb53485-e115-4469-b90a-37da957d322c","uuid":"59ba4d36-a68d-44a2-b413-bb21f3bda792","timestamp":"2026-11-23T02:57:18.399Z","request_id":"req_011ChK7GVkNnadoCcv5VvGpM"}
